:: Roya's full history [scene draft] {"position":"250,1150","size":"100,100"}
Revealed when Roya affects the main plot. Born Mzayyinah, a hereditary status tied to beautifying and service trades, without tribal lineage, with stigma across generations. A young man from a tribal family loves her; his relatives threaten them, since marriage across the line is taboo. They flee and are stopped at a checkpoint. The sheikh hears them and adopts her; the marriage can go ahead.
On the wedding day a gang attacks: the sheikh is killed, the husband abducted and beaten. The sheikh's nephew and villagers fight them off. The couple marry and leave for Old Sana'a. Rent, water, power and medical bills eat their savings; his injuries kill him later. Grieving and poor, Roya joins or aids the rebels.
Proposal: the gang attacks for its own reason (a water route); later Numan's government uses the fear to seize that route.


:: Bilqis's signal or payload [scene idea] {"position":"400,1150","size":"100,100"}
Her catastrophic capability may be access to a legacy emergency network or a prepared release system, not a spell. Rydan might give access, protect her, or supply a physical key. It has a site, prerequisites, risk to allies and a way to stop it.
Ethical core: she may stop a system-wide attack only by starting a system whose effects she cannot predict; Rydan may contain, redirect or shut it down. Not yet a confirmed plot mechanism.


:: Grandfather's grave [scene draft] {"position":"550,1150","size":"100,100"}
Much later: Rydan finds his grandfather's grave and restores the family house; the 3-slot heirloom hilt is a story achievement, not loot. Later still, he chains all three jambiyas into one weapon.

[[Jambiya hilts]]


:: Slot contents [question idea] {"position":"100,1400","size":"100,100"}
Open (Feras, 2026-10-26): gemstones with a tech reason, or tech modules, or anything that makes sense.
Idea from Claude: Yemeni agate (aqiq) cut as crystal cores. Real crystals like quartz turn pressure into current and hold a steady vibration (that is how watches keep time), so a cut core can tune the blade's edge vibration, store a charge or focus a sensor. Old jewellery craft meets tech; a stone's colour shows its job.
Other option: plain engineered modules (capacitors, sensors, edge treatments).
First playable (2026-10-26): the prison hilt's one slot stays empty; this stays open for Feras.
